This version avoids a priority queue. For each source city, it relaxes every undirected edge up to `n - 1` times. If one full pass makes no change, the loop can finish early.

Bellman-Ford is slower than Dijkstra for these positive weights, but the code is easy to check and still works because the graph has no negative edges.

**Dry run:** if a direct road has length `10`, but a two-road path has lengths `4` and `3`, the repeated passes change the saved distance from `10` to `7` before counting reachable cities.

